---

Karis did not go away from the third table.

She stood at its corner, where a square pale hand had rested, and looked down at the fourth of the Hall's twelve. Cael came and stood beside her. He had left the box against a bay. The carried lamp burned at the table's end, a yard from them, where Lira had set it down as carefully as an egg; and the other, on its iron arm, lit a grey head that did not move.

He looked at the sheet, since she was looking at it. He saw what he had seen under a marsh, by a lantern, with water to his knees: rows of small cut signs, drawn square, in her hard clean pencil. He could not have said why a man should stop at that one. He was reading it the wrong way up, from the far side of the table, as he read everything; and it came to him that he stood at the sheet's foot, where the man himself had begun.

"He knows the hand," he said, very low. "He's got something on that shelf that's cut the same. He looked at it, and he came back, and he said so. That's not a mason's pattern-book."

"No."

"He cleared three tables for you. He went without his dinner."

"I saw that he did."

"Then what is it?"

She was shaking again. It was not as she had shaken in a hayloft, with a card on her knee. He saw her take hold of it, a breath at a time, and put it down.

[[TIER-B: "Being believed — " … "It's the end of being able to be wrong." — the plan quotes this unit with an elision and does not pin its speaker; staged here as Karis's answer to Cael at the Hall's table; coordinator to supply protected text]]

He had no answer for her. He had lain awake over this day, as she had, and he had been afraid on her account of one thing only: that a man would look at three floors and shake his head. It had not come into his mind to be afraid of the other.

Brom had come up on her far side. He had a loaf out of his box, and Lira's knife, and he cut it against his chest into five, and laid a piece on the bare wood at the table's very corner, a long way from any paper.

"Eat that," he said. "Then say what you want of us. He'll not say. He doesn't say things."

"How long's it going to take him?" said Lira. She had sat down on the floor with her back against a table's leg and her right hand open on her knee. She was working the fingers. "I only want to know how many days I'm a lamp."

"I do not know. I do not believe that he knows." Karis took up the bread, and looked at it, and set it down. "I have one man who can see how a thing stands, and he has seen it for two hours. I do not yet know what he saw. Whatever it was, I shall have to lay it against all that I have, sheet by sheet; and I shall not be hurried over it, by him or by myself."

She turned round to them. She was the smallest person in that gallery, and she stood with her back to fifty-two sheets, as she had once stood at a broker's plank with a hide.

"Give me a week."

Brom had the figures before she had shut her mouth.

"The loft's paid to the two hundred and eighty-eighth. A week from today wants two marks more, and I've got them. It's seven days of the hill, both ways." He looked at a bound leg. "And of that stair."

"I shall take them as they come," said Vastin. "I believe that I have said so."

He stood a little apart, by the nearest bay. He had been looking at it, Cael saw, for most of two hours, and he had not put out a hand to it. There were tags on some of those bundles after all, on the under side, where a string went through; and the old man's fingers had gone toward one, and stopped a hand's breadth off, and gone back to the ash.

"You'd want me to say it," said Cael. They were looking at him; they had been doing so since a plank at Lowmarch. "It's not a slip. There's nothing to take or leave. She's asked for a week, and it's hers."

"A week," said Brom. "It's priced."

Karis picked up the bread again. She ate it standing, with her eyes on the dark end of the passage that they had come down, where the black stone was.

"I can give you one thing today," she said, when it was gone. "It is small. The rock was cut first. The dark stone was laid against the cut, which makes it second. And what the town has built on the top of both, in mortar, is third." She brushed a crumb from her glove. "That is three kinds of work, one upon another, and I have had my hand on all three since the second bell. I cannot put a year to any of them."

"It's your tree," said Brom. "With the rings."

"It is the tree. I am standing inside it."

---

Cael went down the gallery by himself, away from both lamps.

Nobody had forbidden it. There was a term against a carried light, and he carried none; and a man with shut eyes had said that they might do as they liked, so long as it was quiet. He walked on the rock between the black uprights, counting, and the two flames drew together behind him into one. At forty strides the bundles on either hand had lost their edges. At sixty he could tell the shelves only as a person tells the walls of his own room at night, and he stopped there, and stood.

He did not at once know what it was that he was listening for.

He had been in a great many buildings since he was fourteen, and in every one of them there had been a drawer. There was a counter at Denvash, with a pen on it. There was a house on a bluff, where a file had grown as thick as a man's hand; there was a district seat, and a borrowed office, and a counter in every town between them. He had learned to feel for the drawer on any threshold, as a dog feels for the one board in a floor that gives. Even on the kind side of the Line there had been a book, kept by a man with one arm; that one had been honest, and he had been glad to stand in it, and it had put him eleventh all the same.

He stood among those shelves, and felt for it, and there was nothing to find.

Paper rose on both sides of him to the roof, more of it than a hall above Ostrand had held, and not a leaf of the whole had a line for what he was. It could not have had. Whoever had tied those tapes had done it before there was a plate to stand a child on, or a word in square brackets to put after his name; he had that on the word of a small pale woman, who did not give her word lightly, and of a man who had gone without his dinner.

He drew a breath of the dry cold, and let it go.

It was a strange thing to find in himself. He would have expected to feel light; and he did, a little, as he had felt at a hedge when a rule was lifted off a road. But he did not know how to stand. He had braced against that drawer for so long that his shoulders had forgotten any other way to hang, and he stood between two walls of other people's records with his hands open and nothing to set them against.

It was a hole under a town. He made himself say so. Five storeys of the oldest registry on the Line sat upon its roof. A station stood above that, and a man who kept a green book would let him up at the bell, to be a quarter of a figure again. He was not free of anything. He was only, for the length of a breath, in a place that had no means of knowing him.

He stayed there for a good deal longer than a breath.

---

It was a sound that brought him round. It was small and flat, like a spoon struck once on a cup, and it came from the stair.

"That's him," said the voice like a hinge, from the far end, without stirring. "He pulls a wire. You've a quarter of an hour before he shuts his yard."

They roped the boxes and went up. The second was lighter by fifty-two sheets, and Lira said that she could tell. The man came behind them as far as the iron, and drew the bar, and stood back against the wall with his eyes on their boots; and the clerk was waiting in the vault with his lantern, exactly where he had been left, as though he had not moved since the morning.

The cut was harder going up. Vastin went first, since the slowest must set the pace, and Cael went two steps below him, where a man would fall. On the bottom flight a tread rocked under Brom's weight, and he stopped, and trod on it again to be sure.

"That wants bedding," he said. Nobody answered him, since there was nobody whose business it was.

It was dusk in the lane under the wall. The four alleys had lost their white. There was a paleness at the far end of each now which did not come from any sky, and it lay along the cobbles in a bar that did not flicker. Cael walked through all four with an old man's breath labouring at his shoulder; and a box went by on his left hand each time, between him and it, at the height of a girl's head.

Seln was at the ladder's foot when they came in under the arch. He looked the five of them over as a man counts his change.

"Three people came to look at that low door in the yard, when it had shut on you," he said. "Two were yard-men, and they looked as people look at a thing which they have never seen used. The third was the clerk. He came out upon his own sill, with his book under his arm, and stood there for as long as it takes to read a page. Then he went in again to his hatch and sat down."

"And did what?" said Cael.

"Nothing whatever. I believe that he was pleased."

---

## CHAPTER — editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md

# Chapter 28 — Be Nobody

The second card took them through two gates, on the two hundred and eighty-fifth day, a good deal more easily than the first.

The keepers in blue had seen it now. One of them said *lower archive* to the other, as a man says the name of a bird that he saw once and has now seen twice; then the feather of his pen went along five caps without stopping at any. The old keeper at the round arch read the seal from its stamp inward, exactly as before, and said *one, and four*, and told them again to mind their lame one on the steps.

Cael went down the lane with his eyes on Brom's heels. The four alleys were white again, in the forenoon, and a box went by between him and each of them.

In the cut the third tread of the bottom flight rocked under Brom, as it had rocked the evening before.

"Still wants bedding," he said.

"You could bed it," said Lira.

"It's not my stair."

The yard was as it had been: a wagon at the dock, and a man with a slate at the beam, and nobody who looked round. There was a porter on the bench under the hatch, at its far end, a small man with a carrying-frame between his knees and his cap pulled down, asleep. Cael gave him the glance that he gave to a post. He had been told, on a ladder's head, not to look twice at anything in that yard which was not a door.

Karis laid the card on the ledge. The shutter was up already, and the clerk sat sideways within, with the green book open under his hand.

He counted the boxes with his eyes, as he had the day before, and he dipped his pen. Then he stopped.

It was a small thing to see. The pen had gone down to the leaf, and it did not write. It hung a finger's breadth above a clean line, and the clerk looked at the three boxes on the paving, and at the tarred case, and then he laid his left hand on the book and turned it back by one leaf.

"This is entered," he said.

Cael had not heard him speak before; it was a thin voice, dry and exact, with a short stop after each thing, as though the words were being taken one at a time out of a drawer.

"Three boxes, corded. One surveyor's case, tarred. One courier's flat. One hide, rolled. Brought down for study on the two hundred and eighty-fourth." He read it from his own line, with his finger under it. Then he looked up, at the small pale woman on the other side of his ledge; and it was the first thing that he had ever asked any of them. "The same?"

"The same," said Karis.

"Then they went up again."

"At your bell."

"I know that they did. I let you out; I saw them go by me." He did not sound angry; he sounded like a man who has found a pebble in a dish of peas. "The form is for matter brought down. It has a line for that, and I filled it yesterday, and it was right. It has no line for matter which has gone up and come back."

Nobody answered him, since Karis had been told to give that man nothing which he did not ask for, and he had not asked.

"If I enter them today, my book will say that there are six boxes below, and two cases. There are three, and one." The pen had begun to tremble a very little. "It would not be so. I have kept this book for a great many years, and there is not a line in it that is not so."

He laid the pen down in its tray.

"I shall have to send up. The under-registrar keeps the schedules, and he will know how it is to be put. He will want to see the matter, and the card, and whoever has brought them." He was reaching already for a slip of paper. "He is very thorough. I should not think that it would take him above an hour."

Cael's stomach went cold.

He saw the whole of it, as he would have seen a bad landing a stride before his foot came down. An officer of the registry would come down four flights with a clerk behind him. He would not count. He would look at each of them in turn, as an officer must, and he would have a pen; and the first thing that any such man had ever said to Cael, on any floor, was *and you are?*

They had come two hundred and eighty-five days to that hatch, and got through it once on a figure. It was going to be shut on them by an honest man, who would not write six for three.

---

He did not see the old man move.

He heard the stick, twice, sharp on the paving, and then a brown coat had gone past him and stood at the ledge. He knew that walk. He had last seen it cross a room on a bluff, with a file under its arm; it had four feet of table in front of it then, and every clerk in the building upon his feet.

Vastin knew the ruling. Cael understood that in the same breath, and too late. There was not a schedule in the code that the man could not have said from memory, with its article, and for forty years he had been the person sent for when a clerk could not tell how a thing was to be put. A clerk was in difficulty over a form, six feet away from him. He had gone to it as a healer goes to a bench.

His back was perfectly straight, and he did not look at his boots.

"Vastin. Archmarshal, Compact enforcement. You'll find the name is —"

The clerk's hand had gone to his pen. It was not done on purpose, any more than the other thing had been; a man who has sat at a hatch all his life hears a name and a rank and an office, in their proper order, and his fingers go for the ink. The pen was up, and it was coming over the leaf toward the margin, where a registry writes whatever it is told at its own door.

The porter at the end of the bench had stood up.

Cael had not known him, and he knew him now by the way that he rose, which made no more noise than a coat sliding from a chair. Seln had the cap in his left hand and the frame held by one strap in his right. He was three strides from the ledge, in the plain light of a registry's yard, with a clerk's eyes coming round to him; and he had told five people at a pump that this was the one place in the town where he could not afford to be seen.

He spoke to the clerk, and not to the old man, in the mild voice in which he would have told a carter the hour.

[[TIER-B: Seln: "He's on the count … the form asks what a hand's for, not what he was." — the plan quotes this unit with an elision; staged as Seln's answer to the clerk at the hatch, cutting across Vastin's unfinished sentence; coordinator to supply protected text]]

The pen stopped.

It hung over the margin, with a bead of ink gathered at its point, and for the space of three breaths nothing in that corner of the yard moved at all. Behind them a carter was backing a mule to the dock, and telling it under his breath what he thought of it.

The clerk looked at the small grey man who had spoken. He looked at him properly, from the cap to the boots, as he had not looked at any of the four; and Cael watched a face go into a memory that kept things. Then the clerk looked at the old one, who stood with his mouth still a little open upon a word that he had not been let to finish.

He lifted the pen away from the leaf, and carried it, very steadily, clear of the book's edge, and touched its point to the cuff of his right sleeve, where there was ink already. The bead went into the grey cloth.

"A hand," he said, to nobody. He turned his eyes down to his own line of the day before, and his lips moved over it. "Brought down. For study." He looked at the three boxes once more. Something eased in his face then, slowly, as a knot eases when the right end is found.

He dipped, and wrote two words under yesterday's entry, and the day after them. Cael read them the wrong way up, across the ledge. *The same.* The pen went to the narrow column, and made its single mark; and it went on to the narrower one at the edge of the leaf.

He wrote *four*, and blotted it.

"It needn't go up," he said. There was a kind of wonder in the thin voice. "It is the same matter. I can write that it is the same."

The shutter came down, and a key turned in the low door.

Seln had not lied.

Cael went over it, standing there with a box on his back, since he could not help going over a thing. Every word that the man had said would have stood under two hands. He had told a clerk what was true of a count and of a form. He had not said that an old porter was wandering in his wits, or that it was a carrier's joke, or that the fellow had once swept an Archmarshal's stair and liked to play at it; and any of the three would have shut that clerk's ears a great deal faster than what he did say. He had left the thing that the old man had put upon the ledge lying where it was put, and it was true, and a clerk of the registry now had it.

He had paid for it in the only coin that he owned.

For fifteen years no desk had been able to say what Seln looked like, and that was the whole of his trade. A clerk remembered furniture, and he had taken care to be furniture that was never spoken to; he had kept off a card yesterday for exactly that. Today he had stood up in a yard, and shown his face to a man who forgot nothing, and joined it with his own mouth to four porters and a card. He would not sit on that bench again, and Cael did not think that he would so much as walk through that yard.

The door had opened. The clerk stood on its sill, with the book shut under his arm, and he looked at Vastin once, as a man looks at an inkwell that has gone over and has by some mercy missed the page. Then he stood aside.

Karis went in, and Brom, and Lira. The old man had not stirred from the ledge; his two raw hands were on the head of the stick, and they were shaking.

"I'm sorry," Vastin said.

"Don't be sorry," Seln said. "Be nobody."

He put his cap on. He swung the frame to his shoulder, as a porter does who has given up waiting for a load, and walked away down the yard toward the carts' gate, without haste, among the wagons; and he did not look back at a door.

---

The lamp had gone to Brom that morning, and it went down the stair in front of him.

Cael went below the stick, as he had the day before. The man in the dusty coat had counted three boxes and a case at the iron, with his eyes on the floor, and gone ahead of them into the dark; he had seen nothing in an old porter's face, since he did not look at faces.

On the first landing the stick stopped.

The light went on down. Cael watched it sink, a tread at a time, with Brom's bent back above it and two shadows following, until the low roof of the second flight took it and there was only a greyness on the wall. He stood on the landing's lower edge and waited, and he could hear the old man's breath, which was not the breath of a knee.

"I gave him the whole of it," said Vastin.

He said it to the wall.

"The name, the rank, and the office, in the order of the roll. I did not decide to do it. I heard it as a man hears a clock strike in his own house; and by the time that I knew what the sound was, it had struck three." The stick's foot grated on the stone. "He had his pen up. He would have written it. It would have stood in that book tonight, under a card with one name on it, and gone up to a counter by the fourth bell."

"He'd begun."

"He had. I have watched ten thousand clerks begin."

Cael said nothing for a moment. He could have told the man that it had come to no harm, and it would have been true by the width of a pen's point, and neither of them would have been the better for it.

"It wasn't him sending up that did it," he said. "You'd stood that. You stood a whole gate of it yesterday, twice. It was that you knew the answer."

"I knew the article." It came out before the old man could stop it, and he heard it, and was silent. Then, more slowly: "Yes. He had a difficulty with a form, and I have never in my life stood by while a clerk had a difficulty with a form. I had the schedule in my mouth. I was three words from telling him its number."

"And then he'd have asked who was telling him."

"I told him before he asked. I have always found it quicker."

There was something in that which was very nearly a joke, and it was a good deal worse than if he had not made it.

Cael looked at the dark where a brown coat was, and he knew what he was looking at, which he had not expected to.

He had had a name once that was only a name. A plate had put a word after it, in a building with no windows; and from that day he had not been able to give the one without the other arriving behind it, like a dog behind a cart. So he had learned to say the word himself. He had said it first, and flatly, across every counter that he came to, and looked the clerk in the eye while he did it, so that it should cost the man something to write. It had been the only blade that he owned. He had carried it for longer than he liked to count; and he had laid it down for the first time yesterday, at that same hatch, and been a pair of boots.

This man's name had been a key: it had opened every door on the continent for forty years, and at each of them somebody had been standing ready with a book. It opened nothing now; it was a knife that had lost its haft, and there was no way left of taking it up that did not cut whoever stood nearest.

"I used to say mine first," said Cael. "The word they put after it. I thought that if I got it out before they did, it was mine and not theirs."

"And was it?"

"No. It only saved them the trouble."

The stick shifted.

"I looked at my boots yesterday," Cael said. "At that ledge, with his pen over the line. I'd never done it. Not for a registrar or a warden, or for you. It was the hardest thing I've done in a yard, and I've been carried out of one."

"I observed that you did it."

"I know. You were doing it beside me."

"I was doing it for the second day, and I did it rather well, and I had begun to think it learned." The breath went out of him. "It appears that a thing is not learned until it has been tried by the one matter that you care for. I can keep my tongue while I am counted. I had not yet been asked to keep it while a form was filled wrongly."

"He didn't fill it wrongly. He worked it out."

"He did. He needed nobody." There was a long pause. "That is the part which I shall be some time over."

Far below them the lamp had come out into the passage, and somebody had turned to look back up the stair; Cael could see a small bright point at the bottom of the three flights, and a shape beside it that was a girl.

"He told me what to be," said Vastin. "He did not tell me how it is done."

"He told you at a spring. You look at what's there. You leave your hands where they are." Cael felt for the next tread with his heel. "That's all I've ever had of it. It's nine steps to the next landing. I'm below you."

"So you are," said the old man; and the stick came down.

---

The second chair had been cleared.

Cael saw it from the gallery, over Brom's shoulder, before the man at the desk had turned his page. There were two chairs at that desk, of which one stood before the slope, and had a cushion on it, flattened by more years than he could guess into the shape of one person. The other stood against the wall at the desk's side. It had been heaped yesterday, like every flat thing in that place, with bundles to the height of its back; and it was bare now, and somebody had wiped it.

Karis saw it, and did not sit; she stood by it with the flat under her arm until the grey head came round.

"Sit," said the man. "I'll want you answering, and I'll not have you swaying over my shoulder. Lamp."

Brom brought it.

"Not the wall. I said so."

So it went to Lira, and she held it for an hour in her right hand, with her elbow on her knee; and at the end of the hour the flame had begun to shiver, by so little that Cael could tell it only from the edge of a shadow on the Ring's third sheet. She looked at her own wrist as though it had spoken out of turn.

Vastin's eyes had gone to the ring. He could not kneel, and he had one hand for the stick, and he knew both of those things; Cael saw him know them, and look away.

"Give it here," said Cael.

He had not planned it; he took the ring out of her fingers before the man could say that it had moved.

He went down on his left knee, a hand to the left of the place where two red eyes were resting, and laid his right forearm along his thigh, and let the lamp hang from two fingers as a mason lets a plumb-bob hang. Then he did the one thing that he knew how to do in such a case: he let his breath go long. He counted it, as a healer had taught him on a plank, to ten and to ten again, and he gave over wanting to be anywhere else.

He knew how to be a post, which was most of what he had learned since a plate lit. He had stood still in the corners of halls so that he should not be looked at, and at the edges of yards. He had stood locked on a bank of scree for a whole count, with six hounds coming and not a finger that would answer him. He had never before been asked for it as a skill.

The flame stood up like a painted one.

Nothing was said for a long while. The man went along the Ring's eleven again, and he did it otherwise than yesterday: he did not go sheet by sheet now, but across them, from a mark on one to a mark on its neighbour, with his head moving a little from side to side. When he moved, Cael moved, a hand to the left. He found that he could tell where the eyes would go next by the set of the shoulders.

"This edge," the man said, to the chair by the wall. "Did the stone break there, or did the cutting stop?"

"It stopped."

"Clean?"

"With a border. A double line, cut shallower than the signs."

"Hm."

That was all that he gave her for it. He had asked how a thing was made, and been told, and what he had made of the telling went nowhere that Cael could see. Karis wrote nothing; she sat forward on a bare chair with her gloved hands shut, and watched him as she would have watched a pot that she was not allowed to stir.

Somewhere among the Stair's twenty-nine he stopped, and straightened his back with a hand in the small of it.

"That'll do," he said. "Leave it with that one."

Lira, on the floor by the table's leg, looked up.

"He means you."

"He means the lamp," said Cael, without moving his lips more than he must.

He did, for the red eyes had not come within a yard of the hand that held the ring, and they never did. When the man wanted the light he said *lamp*, and when he wanted it elsewhere he said *left*, or *up a finger*, and it went; he took no more notice of what moved it than a clerk takes of the hook on which his coat is hung.

"Thirty years I've carried that myself," the rough voice said, a while after, to the sheet in front of it. "One hand for the light, and half an eye for where I'd put it. I'd forgotten what it was to have the two of them together."

Cael knelt on cold rock and turned that over. He had been a name with a word after it for the whole of his life since his Kindling. Yesterday he had been the quarter part of a figure; and today he was a lamp. It was a rise, of a sort; a lamp was at least good at something.

And he was two feet from it. He had wished, in a wood-yard, to see how that man looked at a thing. He saw it now for hours together, from closer than Karis sat, with every sheet lying foot toward him and wrong way up, as he had read every paper of his life. The eyes began where the weight came down, and they asked the pencil for nothing that it had not offered. He could not see what they found; he could only see that they found it.

The bell went at noon, somewhere above, too far to hear; but the man heard his own stomach, and put both hands on the table.

"I'm going up for an hour. Set it on the table's end. Touch nothing." He went by them toward the stair with his eyes already half shut. "There's a jar of water on the shelf. You may have what's in it."

When his steps had gone, Brom walked up the gallery and stood looking at the two chairs, the cushioned and the bare.

"That's thirty years of one man," he said, of the cushion. "It's the shape of him. I'd not fit it."

"It'd not fit you," said Lira. "It's had a lot less to do."

"He cleared the other," said Karis. She had not got up from it. She laid one glove flat on the wiped wood, beside her knee. "He did that last night, after we had gone up. I do not suppose that anybody has sat here in his lifetime."

Nobody said anything to that. Brom cut the loaf.

---

Seln was in the loft before them, sitting on the ladder's head with his feet on the second rung.

He had no frame. He had given it back to the man that he hired it of, and paid the copper; and he looked exactly as he had looked on every other evening, which Cael thought must have taken some doing.

"I shall not sit on that bench again," he said, before anybody could begin. "There is a roof above the cut's head, on the last building, with a parapet to it. I can see the yard from there, and the door, and the whole of the stair; and nobody looks at a roof."

"He saw you," said Lira. "That clerk. I watched him do it. He looked at you like a thing that he was going to draw."

"He did. He will know me in ten years, in another town, in a different coat."

The old man had come up the ladder last, on one leg and two hands, and he stood at the gable under the blanket and had not yet let go of the stick.

"I have said that I am sorry. I am told that it is the wrong coin." His voice was level, and it cost him to keep it so. "I shall say then only what was done. You had kept your face from every desk in the Compact for fifteen years, and I spent it for you in a breath, to save a clerk an hour."

"You spent nothing. I stood up. It was my own leg that I did it with."

"You might have had it cheaper," said Cael.

He had not meant to ask; but it had gone round in him through four hours of holding a lamp, and he wanted it out.

"There were things you could have said that'd have shut him up for good. That he was simple. That he'd been somebody's servant once and it took him that way. He'd have believed you; he wanted to. And he'd not have looked at you twice."

"I had three such, and each was quicker." Seln put his hands into his sleeves. "I told this loft, three nights ago, that I would not have a lie told at a registry's door by anybody who means to go on standing on this side of the Line. I did not then except myself. I found this morning that I had not meant to."

"That's a dear way to be right," said Brom.

"It is the only way that I have found which stays right afterward."

Nobody added to it. Brom went down for the pot, and brought it up on a board, with seven bowls, and set the seventh at the end by itself; and the old man took his standing and thanked him once.

Shadow lifted while they ate, and Cael felt every other sound in him sink, as a yard goes quiet under snow, and he told it no, and it lay down.

He took out Hesk's book when the bowls had gone, and set the day at its head.

*Two hundred and eighty-fifth day. Treswick; the loft. A hundred and thirty-six. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two. Lamp, from about the third hour. He says left and I go left.*

He looked at that last line, and let it stand, since it was the first use that anybody had found for him inside a wall. Then he wrote the rest, and it came all in one piece, as it did when he had carried a thing the whole day.

*Karis has been looking for this room her whole life. Cleon's been sitting in it for thirty years waiting for her. Vastin tried to open a door with his rank and nearly closed all of them — an Archmarshal's worth nothing here, which he's taking about as well as I took [SHATTERED]. Down here the stone's older than the Compact, so the Compact doesn't reach. First place inside walls I've ever stood where nobody can file me, and it's a hole under a city, and it's because everything down here is older than the machine that files. Karis says give her a week. I've got a box to carry and a promise to keep about a station upstairs. I can wait.*

He turned the book round. Lira read it with her chin down and her lips going, and stopped at the third line, and looked across the lantern at a brown coat by the gable.

"That's not well," she said, very low. "You've put that he's taking it as you did. You took it like a cat takes a bath."

"I know how I took it."

"He said sorry. Out loud, in a yard, to the one of us he'd most cause to be afraid of." She gave him the book. "You never said it to anybody."

"There wasn't anybody to say it to."

"No," said Lira. "That was the trouble with it." She lay down on her pallet, on the right side, with the slow arm uppermost. "I held that lamp an hour. My hand's still going. How do you do it?"

"I stop wanting to be anywhere."

"That's horrible."

"It's useful," said Cael; and he shut the book on the pencil, and found that he meant both.

---

## CHAPTER — editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md

# Chapter 29 — The Cart and the Board

The dray came into the yard behind them, on the two hundred and eighty-sixth day, by the carts' gate at the bottom.

Cael marked it only because it was the heaviest thing there. It had two wheels as tall as Lira, and one mule between the shafts, and it was loaded to the rails with roofing slate, stacked on edge in straw. A lad of twelve or so walked at the mule's head. A man in a good grey cloak and a broad hat walked beside the tail-board, with a tally in his hand; and there was a square of slate painted on the dray's side, which was some broker's sign.

Loads came into that yard all day, and Cael had learned in two mornings how it took them. Whatever had wheels came up from the bottom, past the beam, where a man with a slate wrote a weight against an owner; whatever had legs came down the slot in the wall, as they had, and was written nowhere until it reached a hatch. The yard sloped between the two a good deal more steeply than a carter would have chosen, and its paving had been worn into one long shallow trough by more iron tyres than anybody had counted.

The four of them had come out of the cut a moment before, and Karis had gone to the hatch with the card. Brom had set his box down by the bench. He was looking at the dray as he looked at any load, to see how it had been put together.

"That's too much for one axle," he said. "On a slope."

It was the near wheel that went. Cael saw the pin jump from its hole and ring on the stones; and after that the wheel walked off its arm, quite slowly, as a drunken man leaves a doorway, and rolled away down the yard by itself.

The dray dropped on that corner; its bed tipped, and the whole stack of slate slid to the low side in one long clattering rush, like a dresser of plates going over. The mule screamed and plunged. The lad had let go of its head and jumped back toward the dock, as anybody would have; and the bed's rail came down across his boot and held it against the kerb.

He did not cry out; he stood bent over, with both hands on the rail, and said the same thing three times in a small surprised voice.

"It's on my foot. It's on my foot."

Two yard-men were running already, with a pole. The man in the cloak was shouting for a lever, and pointing. They went where he pointed, to the high side, where the other wheel stood a foot clear of the ground, and got the pole's end under the axle.

"Not there," said Brom.

He had not raised his voice, and it carried all the same, as a bell carries. He was walking, not running, with his eyes on the heap of slate; and Cael, who had watched him read a wall a hundred times, saw him read this.

"You'll roll it. It's sitting on that corner like a box on one becket. Lift the high side and the whole of it goes over onto him." He had come to the low corner. He crouched, and looked along the under side of the bed, and at the stub of the axle, and at a boot. "Leave that pole. Put it down. All that's holding it off his leg is the slate that slid, and the slate's not done sliding."

It was not. A single plate shifted in the heap while he spoke, and the rail came down by the width of a thumb, and the lad's breath went in through his teeth.

*What does it weigh?* asked the read.

It asked under the soles of Cael's feet, low and even, as it had asked of every rut for nine days; and it asked also which way the weight was going, since that was the half of its trade. It could have told him to a stone. It could have told him which slate would move next.

He told it no. He stood in a registry's yard, twenty feet from a hatch with its shutter up. A girl with eggs had been turned from a gate in that town for the wrong hour on a card; he did not care to learn what was written against a porter who was seen to know a thing that he had no way of knowing.

And Brom had not asked for it; Brom had his head on one side, and was looking.

"Lira. His belt, at the back. Both hands, if you've got them. Not till I say, and when I say, you take him straight toward yourself and you don't stop." He did not look round. "Cael. The wood wagon. Two splits, thick, as long as your arm; and one of those men to drive them. Under the axle first."

"And you?" said Lira.

"I'm going to pick it up."

---

He got down on one knee beside the kerb, and put his left shoulder under the corner of the bed.

It was the good one. The right was in four yards of webbing, under his shirt, with another woman's knot upon it, and he held that arm across his thigh as a man keeps a tool out of the way. He set his left foot flat, and his hand on the stones.

Cael had the splits. He had taken them off the wagon's tail without a word to anybody. A yard-man had come with him, since the big stranger plainly knew what he was about; they knelt at the axle's stub with the wood ready. From there Cael could see the side of Brom's neck.

He watched it for the grey.

He had seen it come up a dozen times: on sprung oak, and in a hall, and on a road with hounds. It began under the collar and went over the skin like frost over a pane, and when it had gone over, a blade would not mark the man. A dray would have been nothing to him then. He would have stood up under it as under a sack.

It did not come. The neck stayed the colour of a neck, and the cords stood out in it.

Brom had a sigil in his pocket that would have made a stone of him. He had a certificate, somewhere, that said *Copper*, with a finding pinned to it that nobody had signed. He was twenty feet from a clerk who wrote down whatever was brought to his ledge; and he took a dray of slate on his bare shoulder, as a carter's son might have, with nothing to help him but his legs.

Cael had priced that man a great many ways since a yard above Ostrand. He had priced him by what he could carry, and by what he would take on his own skin for somebody else, and by a figure that a board had given him and a house had declined to sign; and he saw now that every one of those sums had had the grey in it. He had never once asked what the man was worth with the Path shut. It appeared to be a dray of slate.

"Now," he said, through his teeth.

The corner came up, by no more than two fingers; the slate groaned in the straw, and the rail lifted off a boot.

Lira had the belt in her right hand and the left hooked beside it, a beat behind, as it was behind in everything. She threw her whole weight backward. The lad came out from the kerb like a cork, and sat down on the paving with her; and his boot stayed where it was, under the rail, empty.

"He's clear!"

"I know he's clear. It's shifted."

It had. The lifting had tipped the heap again, and a dozen plates had run down into the corner all together; and what Brom had raised by two fingers was now coming down upon him with a good deal more than he had taken up. Cael saw his knee begin to open. He could not set the thing down. The kerb was still under the rail, where a foot had been, and Lira's hands were on the stones beside it.

"Wood," said Brom.

The yard-man drove the first split under the axle's stub with the heel of his hand, and Cael the second beneath the bed's rail, end on, and kicked it home. The dray settled on them. It creaked, and took the oak a little into its timber, and was still.

Brom came out from under it on his hands, and stood up slowly. He tried to lift the left arm, and it went as high as the elbow would carry it, and stopped; and he looked at it with the deep patient dislike that he had once given to a hound.

"That's gone," he said. "A week. Might be two."

Somebody was saying *easy, easy, stand*, over and over, in a low level voice, and it was not a carter.

Cael turned, and the mule was standing. It had been throwing itself about in the shafts a moment since, and every plunge had rocked the bed. It stood now with its head down and its sides going, and an old man had hold of its cheek-strap. He had the stick in his right hand. The left, which would not open flat, had shut on the leather; shutting was the one thing that it could still do well. He was looking at nobody; he had held a ferry's stern so once, in a flood, with a turn round a post.

He had not given one order. Cael went back over the whole of it to be sure. Three men had been shouting in that yard, and a fourth had been obeyed by all of them; and the one person there who had commanded for forty years had seen a beast that wanted holding, and had gone and held it.

The lad had his toes in both hands, and was counting them aloud. There were five, and they bent.

At the hatch a pen had been going through all of it. Cael looked that way once, between the axle and the bench, and saw the green book open and a grey sleeve moving. It came to him that the clerk had written *the same*, and a figure, and blotted them, while a dray came down ten yards from his ledge, since none of it was matter for the lower archive and he had no line to put it on.

"What tier?"

It was the man in the cloak. He had come round the tail-board, and he stood in front of Brom with his hat brim down, so that Cael saw of him only a shaved chin and a pair of clean hands. The voice was a quick, dry, townsman's one.

"Copper," said Brom.

"A Copper would have done that with the grey on him, and I'd have thought it no more than his work. You did it in your shirt." The chin lifted an inch. "I watched your neck. You never lit."

"It's a registry's yard."

"So it is. My master keeps eleven on his doors, and there's not one of them will shift a keg until he's lit up to do it, in front of whoever's passing; and then it's a line in somebody's book, every time." The clean hands turned the tally over. "The house is the third on the brokers' street, with the slate above its door. There is a guard's post at the store we keep inside the second wall. It's a standing pass, sealed, by the month. It's a bed in the house, above the wall, and the house's coat. Four marks, and found."

Nobody spoke. Lira had got up off the paving.

It was a real offer, and Cael knew what it weighed. There was a house in Velmere that had wanted exactly this for its son, for as long as it had had him: a coat with somebody's colour on it, and a place inside a wall, and a figure at the month's end that could be written in a letter. A panel had sat once to tell that house what he was worth. Here a stranger had told him in a yard, after watching his neck.

Brom looked at the dray, propped on two logs. He looked at the hatch, where a small pale woman stood with a card, and had not once turned her head.

"I'll think on it."

"Ask for the factor."

The man touched his hat, and went to see to his slate.

Brom walked back to the bench, and bent to his box, and put his left hand to the becket, and the arm would not take it. He stood a moment; then he turned his other side to it, and swung the box up, in one heave, onto the right shoulder, over the webbing.

"That's the one she tied," said Lira.

"It's a box."

"She said that if it told you anything—"

"It's not said a word. Cart was heavier."

Cael had his mouth open, and found that there was nothing in it that would serve. He was the one who ruled on slips. He could not rule on a shoulder; it had never been his, and the man under it was looking at him with perfect mildness.

"It goes in the book," he said.

"Put it in. Put in that it carried."

---

Lira went below the stick that morning, on the stair inside the iron.

She had said that she would, at the door, with the lamp in her hand; and she had looked at Brom's right shoulder as she said it, and then at Cael. So he came down last but one, with nothing to do but watch a box ride where a healer had said that nothing was to ride. It sat well. Brom carried it as he would have carried it on the other side, and if the knot spoke to him, he did not pass on what it said.

They were on the second flight, with the light a long way beneath, when the big man spoke.

"You think Karis is right? That it's all built on something?"

Cael took a tread before he answered.

"I think we're about to find out."

"Good. I'd hate to have turned down a real job for a wrong hunch."

Cael missed a tread, and caught it.

"You told him you'd think on it."

"I did think on it. I thought on it from the dray to the bench." The box shifted. "It's a fair post. I'd have taken it at sixteen and written home the same night."

"And now?"

"Now I've sixty yards of rope to buy going out, and a bowl to carry to a mill." He went down three steps. "He counted my neck. I've been counted by better; Pike never asked me what I was. Mind that landing."

That was all of it. Cael had been afraid, on a climbing street, of a blue coat that would read a girl's name aloud. He had not thought to be afraid of a grey cloak; and it had come and gone in a yard, while he knelt at an axle, and he had not been asked to do anything about it at all.

There was something new at the third table.

He saw it from the gallery's mouth, with the carried lamp still thirty strides ahead of him: a long dark thing stood on its end against the table's edge, beside the Hall's fourth sheet, with a fold of sacking on the floor at its foot. It was a plank. It was of oak, as black as the shelves, as long as a man's arm and two hands broad; and as the light came up he saw that one face of it had been cut.

Karis had stopped a yard from it, with both arms full, and she did not go nearer.

There were four rows. They ran across the board's width, small and square and deep, and he knew them as a person knows the letters of his own tongue in a strange hand: he had seen their kind on three floors, by lantern, in her pencil. At the end of each row the cutter had made something else, a cluster of little pecks and rings; and after the cluster, a few signs more.

"That is not paper," she said, hardly above a breath.

"No," said the voice from the desk. A leaf was turned face down. "It was on the bottom shelf of the west gallery, under a heap of quarrymen's wedges, with its face to the wall. I've had it ten years." The chair scraped. "Lamp."

Cael took the ring from Lira, and went down on his knee, a hand to the left of a plank, and let his breath go long.

---

The man had three bowls on the table's end, and a reed.

The bowls held counters: white pebbles in one, and black in another, and in the third a heap of small red squares that had been broken from a tile. The reed was as long as his forearm. He did not touch paper with his skin, and Cael had seen by now that he did not mean to; the reed did it for him, from a hand's breadth off.

"Top row of that sheet," he said to the chair. "I'll point. You lay. White where I say white."

He pointed, and Karis laid. A white pebble went down on the first sign of the row. Three black followed it, on the next three. Then red, and red; and white again; and black. She set each one below its mark, not upon it, so that her own pencil should not be hidden. It went the length of the row, twelve signs, and when it was done there were twelve counters in a line across the foot of her drawing: two white, seven black, three red.

"Now the board."

The reed went to the plank's first row. He said the colours again, and she laid them along the table's edge beneath it; and Cael, who had the best seat in the gallery for it, saw the second line of pebbles grow beside the first. White. Three black. Red, and red.

They were one line, laid twice.

Karis saw it at the fourth counter; her hand stopped in the bowl, and went on.

"What's the white?" said Lira, from the floor.

"It bears." The reed touched the air over a white pebble. "Take that out and the row comes down. There's nothing left for the rest to hang on."

"And the black?"

"Hangs. It's carried. You could change one of those for another kind, and the row'd still stand up; it'd only be carrying something different." The reed moved. "The red's nothing. It holds nothing and nothing holds it. It's there so that the row fills its stone from edge to edge, as a mason packs a joint."

"How d'you know?" said Brom. He had come to stand at the table's far corner, with his right arm folded and his left hanging. "They're scratches. One's as deep as the next."

"How do you know which wall of a house you can put a door through?"

Brom thought about it honestly.

"You go and look at what's sitting on it."

"That's the whole of it. I look at what's sitting on what." The red eyes had not left the board. "I've a Path that lets me look a good deal harder than you can. That's all the difference there is between us, and it's the dullest one on the roll."

Cael knelt two feet from that Path, and felt nothing whatever.

He had stood beside Brom when the grey came up, and beside a wall on a moor that had roared like a forge with its door open. This made no sound at all. The man only looked, from the foot of a thing upward, without blinking, while the water ran down beside his nose; and whatever it was that he did, it went into the oak as quietly as ink goes into paper. It was the stillest thing that Cael had ever known a person do with what a plate had given him.

He could not have said what he had looked for in it. A Path, as he had met them, was a thing that was done to somebody: each of the ten in him had come out of a blow, or a throw, or a pair of hands laid on to mend, and he had learned every one of them by what it cost the person at the other end. This was done to nobody. It was only a man attending, as hard as a man can, to something that another man had made.

The reed had gone back to the sheet, and it went along the white pebbles, the first and the second, and came to rest.

[[TIER-B: "They're orders." — the plan quotes this line whole but does not pin its speaker; staged here as Cleon's, said over the board and the Hall's fourth sheet; coordinator to supply protected text]]

Karis had risen from the chair, and did not seem to know that she had. She stood with a black pebble shut in her glove, and she had gone so white that Brom took a step.

"Karis. Sit down before you go over on his paper."

"I said no names," said the man, sharply.

"I never gave it to you. I gave it to her."

"I heard it."

"Then you'd best forget it."

"I don't forget things. It's why I asked." But he had turned his head, for the first time that morning, a very little toward the chair; and he waited until she was in it again.

"Orders to do what?" said Brom.

"I can tell you these are orders. I can't tell you what they order. For that you need the language, and the language is in the text, not the stone. That's her work, not mine."

He laid the reed down across the bowls.

"A white one stands at the head of every run, and all that follows hangs from it, and goes toward an end. A list doesn't do that. A prayer doesn't. The only writing I've ever met that's built so is the kind that tells a man to do a thing, and what to do it to, and how many times." He rubbed his eyes with the heel of his hand, and it did them no good. "That's the bones. I've no more than bones. I never have had."

Vastin had not moved from the nearest bay. He spoke to the board, and it was plain that he had not meant to speak.

"To do a thing; to what; and how many times." The stick's foot turned on the rock. "I have drawn some thousands of papers in that form, and had as many drawn for me. I have followed fifty-two of them down a stair for three days, on other people's backs; and it did not once enter my mind that I was looking at my own trade."

"You'd not have known it," said Lira. "It's in scratches."

"I should have known the shape. It is the shape that I am supposed to be good at."

"You did the bridge," said Brom. "The grey one, with the five piers. You'll have had a look at that."

"Twice. It was the first thing I did when I came here."

"And the river under it?"

"It doesn't work on a river, and it doesn't work on a man, and it doesn't work on a tree, because nobody chose where the branches went. It costs me my eyes for an hour after and nothing else."

He said it as a carter states his rate, and then he went up for his bowl.

---

Karis counted while he was gone.

She did it without a pebble, on a leaf of her notebook, going from sheet to sheet of the Hall's twelve with the pencil held upright. She had forty-one kinds of sign, and had had them for the better part of a year; and she knew now, of the twelve that stood in one row, which kind bore and which was carried. It did not give her the rest. She said so, twice, to nobody.

"I shall want every row," she said. "On every sheet. He will have to go through all fifty-two with that reed, and I shall have to lay each one and write it; and only then shall I be able to say how many kinds are white."

"That's your week," said Brom.

"It is a good deal of my week."

The man came down within the hour, and it was done as she had said. He went along the Hall's table through the afternoon with the reed, and she laid, and wrote; and between the sheets he went back to the plank. The second of its rows stood on the Stair's ninth sheet, nine signs long, and the third on the Ring's sixth. He found each as a carpenter finds a joint in the dark, by going to where it ought to be. Each time the reed came back to the plank, and the pebbles went down along the table's edge in the order in which they had gone down on paper, white and black and red.

The fourth row was the shortest, of seven signs, and it stood near the foot of the Stair's twenty-first sheet. When he had found it he straightened, and put both hands in the small of his back.

[[TIER-B: "Same make." — the plan quotes this line whole but does not pin its speaker; staged here as Cleon's, of the board and the three floors, after its fourth row is found; coordinator to supply protected text]]

Cael kept the flame where it was wanted, and did not hear what Karis answered. He saw her glove come down upon the table's edge, flat, an inch from her own pencil, where she had been told that nobody was to lean; and the man beside her looked at the glove, and for once did not say so.

He had been listening to another thing since noon. It had been said over his head, to a big man, as a rate is stated; and one piece of it had gone into him like a splinter. It was the piece about a man.

He had come to that town with one question. He had written at a fire that he was about to hand it to a stranger, and had been afraid of it for two days, in a loft, with a small pale woman's roll across his knee. He need not have been. The stranger had told a gallery, without being asked, that he could not take it.

It ought to have been a relief; and he knelt on the rock, and felt for the relief, and it was not there.

He wanted it tried.

He looked at that squarely, with his breath going long and the flame standing up. Two feet from his right hand there was a pair of sore red eyes that could see what held a thing up. They had found it in a cutting that nobody living had made, off a drawing, in the time that it takes to swallow. He had a place under his breastbone that five readings had come to and none had entered. He had been told, in so many words, that those eyes were for walls and seals and boards, and not for boys. He believed it. He wished, all the same, that the grey head would turn two feet to the left and look.

He did not say so, since there was nobody to say it to. The man took no more note of him than of the iron arm that his own lamp hung from, and Cael had asked to be exactly that.

He put the wish where he put a cord, and a hair, and a date. He would write it tonight, and he would write that he had not spoken it.

"Left," said the rough voice.

He was there already.

When the bell went they roped the boxes, and Brom took his on the right shoulder again, and nobody said anything about it. On the long stair Cael counted the steps behind that box, twenty-seven, and the twenty-two after them in the open air; and the big man did not once shift it to the other side, since there was no other side to shift it to.

The dray was gone from the yard when they crossed it. Its two logs lay by the dock, where somebody had thrown them, and the wheel had been found. Brom looked at the place in passing, as a man looks at a piece of work that he has finished and been paid for.

He wrote it in the loft, under the day.

*Brom: the left shoulder wrenched, under a dray in the registry's yard, with no Path opened. He is carrying on the right, in the old healer's knot, against Oryn's word at the fork. I did not tell him to put it down. I couldn't have made him. Owed at the mill, to two healers, by me as much as by him. Entered.*

*Cleon has a board. He put it against her fourth sheet and it was the same line twice. Some signs bear, and some are carried, and some are nothing; she'll not say how many of each until all fifty-two are laid.*

*He says what it won't work on, and I'm one of the things. I wanted it tried all the same. Not said.*

*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*

---

## CHAPTER — editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md

# Chapter 30 — What Seln Looked At

Brom lay on his back that night, since there was no side left to him that he could lie upon.

Lira had begged a pot of something from the stable, which was meant for horses, and smelled of it. She rubbed it into the left shoulder by the lantern, with the heel of her right hand, while he looked at the slates and said that it was a waste of good grease. He did not let her near the right. That one was another woman's knot, he said, and nobody was to lay a finger on it until the woman had.

"You lifted a cart," said Lira.

"I lifted a corner of one."

"Then lie still for a corner."

The old man stood at the gable, under the blanket, as he stood every night until the last of them was down. He had an awl in his fingers again. There was nothing left on the tarred case that wanted stitching, and he had begun on the becket of Cael's box, without asking, where the rope had started to fray.

Shadow had lifted at dusk, in the lane, between the second alley and the third. Cael had told it no while a bar of pale light went over his boots. It lay shut now, and the other nine had come back, each to its own place.

He could not sleep. He had written three words about a wish, and the writing had not put it down.

The lantern went out. He lay with his hands behind his head and listened to the loft, as he had listened to every room that he had ever slept in.

A room full of sleepers has a shape, and he had known this one's for five nights. Brom made the most of it, in the middle, breathing like something felled. Lira lay on her right side, a yard from Cael's elbow, and Karis by the boxes, small and quick, with a catch at the top of every breath that had been there since a cook-house. There was the old man's, upright, by the gable. And there was a place near the ladder's head where nothing was. He had learned to know it as a person knows where a rug lies in a dark room, because the floor does not ring there.

The room changed shape.

He heard nothing. The ladder did not creak; it never had, under that one. There was only a difference in the dark by the ladder's head, as when a coat that has hung on a door for a week is taken down, and the door is seen to be a door.

Seln had gone out.

Cael lay and looked at the slates that he could not see.

He had got up once before, in that loft, and found a grey man sitting on a rung, and asked him for something. He could have gone to the ladder now. He could have looked down into the yard, and seen which way a shadow went; he had Shadow of his own, under a seal, which would have told him more than his eyes.

He did not move.

There was a case. It hung on a strap across that man's chest, and it had hung there on every road since Ostrand, shut. Cael knew what was in it. Five of them had gone through it on a table, by a shaded lamp, on a night above Ostrand; and he had written the next morning that it did not go into a book. It had not gone into this one either.

And they were inside a wall now. They were two hundred yards beneath the oldest registry on the Line; and that case had not been inside a Compact wall since five people went down a bluff with a cart. He did not know what a man did who brought such a thing back within reach of the people that it concerned. He did not think that Seln knew.

A sheet had been laid before him once, at a spring, with its head turned away; and the person who laid it had gone to a basin and stood with his back turned, so that it need never be asked what was made of it. Cael had understood only afterward what that had cost.

He could pay some of it back, by lying where he was.

It was harder than holding a lamp. He stopped wanting to be at the ladder, as he had stopped wanting to be anywhere else on a floor of cold rock, and after a time it held.

---

Seln went down the ladder on the outer ends of the rungs, where the wood does not speak, and crossed the yard in the shadow of the pump.

He did not look up at the loft. He knew that the boy was awake. He had known it by the breathing, which was a little too even, and he had found that he did not much mind. There had been a time when he would have waited an hour for it to change.

The half-door was shut and barred at that hour, and the woman who leaned on it was asleep. He went out by the carriage-arch, past the painted door. Its lamp burned, and the book lay open on its stand within, with a pen on a chain, for whoever came late and had a name to give.

It was a quiet district at night. He had expected that. A town which lets its labour in by the piece does not keep it up after dark; the benches were empty, and the slates had been wiped, and the only wheels that he heard were the watch's handcart, two streets off, going its round with a lantern. He let it go by. Then he went up to the ring road, and along under the wall, as far as the chandler's.

He had marked the roof three days since, with a keg on his back. It was the tallest below the blue gate: a warehouse of four floors, with a hoist-beam at its gable, and an outside stair of wood for the men who worked the hoist. Nobody had thought to put a door on the stair. Nobody ever did.

He went up it, and out along the leads, and sat down with his back against a chimney that was still warm.

The whole hill stood in front of him.

He had not seen it so before. He had gone up it once as a counted hand, with his eyes where a porter's eyes are, on the setts; and he had watched a yard from a bench, and a door from a parapet. From here it was laid out as a plan is. The second wall went round below his feet, with the blue gate in it, shut, and a brazier burning in the arch where two keepers stood their night. Above that the slate roofs climbed, close and dark. Above the slates ran the third wall, the old one, low and pale; and over the wall, like the back of a great book stood on a shelf, the registry.

It was five storeys. He could see three of them. There was a light in one window, high up, where somebody was working late or had forgotten a lamp.

To the left of it, and a little higher, there was the other light.

He looked at that for some time. It was small, at such a distance, and pale, and it did not flicker. Every station that he had ever served under had burned so; a man who works in a registry leaves off seeing it within a month, as he leaves off hearing the clock. He had not been inside a wall with one for longer than he cared to reckon. He found that he saw it again.

The boy had not looked at it once. Seln had watched him not look, from a roof, down the whole length of a lane with four alleys in it, and had thought it very well done.

He put his hand up to the strap.

It weighed something under three pounds. He had known that weight for twelve years, to the ounce, as a man knows the weight of his own arm; which is to say that he had not felt it since the first month. It had gone through three postings on him, and across a Line. It had stood for some months between a researcher's grey boxes, on a shelf, with its key in his waistcoat, and had gone back onto the strap on the night that five people left a hill.

He felt it tonight. It pulled a little on the left side, as a debt does.

He did not go over what was in it. He could have named every sheet, and the order in which they lay, and that was exactly the reason. He had told a boy once that a thing told three times is a thing on its way to being known, and he had kept that case for twelve years by never once describing it to anybody. He had found, a long while ago, that the rule held best if he did not except himself.

It was shut. That was all that he needed to have about it tonight.

He had stood over a spring six days since, with a stolen sheet in his sleeve, and had gone through the three things that his training let him do with a fact. He had not been able to look at the case that morning. He had said to himself that a man cannot hold two closed things up to the light at once; and it had been true, and it had also been convenient.

He had opened the one. He had set it between two hands on a rock, and gone to stand by the water.

This was a night, and he had nothing in his sleeve. He looked down at the dark leather against his coat, squarely, as he would have looked at another man's file.

---

There were three doors to that building, as there are to every registry on the Line, and he had used the third for twelve years.

The front was for names. It faced a flight of steps across a paved square, and whoever went in at it was written. The yard was for loads; he had sat by that one on a bench, and it had cost him his face. And at the side, down a passage off the square's eastern corner, there would be a hatch with a ledge and a lamp over it, where a courier might lay a sealed thing at any hour of the night.

He had passed it three days ago, under a keg. He had not needed to turn his head. He knew how it would be: a slot of light, and a night clerk behind it, half asleep, with a book. The courier lays his packet on the ledge. The clerk enters it by its seal and its weight, and tears off a slip. Neither is permitted to ask the other a question. It is the oldest rule in the service, and the only one that he had ever wholly respected. A registry enters what is laid on its ledge. It may bury the thing afterward, or burn the copy, or hold it for six years in a drawer where nobody will receive it; but for the length of one line, in one book, on one night, the thing has been set down as having come.

He could be at that hatch in a quarter of an hour, if he chose to show a keeper what he was.

He considered it with care, since he distrusted himself on the subject.

A line would be written. *One case, locked. By hand.* It would go up in the morning to a counter. Somebody would open it there, with a key or without one. He could not see past that. He had been able to see past it once; he had known the names of the men who read that sort of return, and what each of them wanted, and which would carry it up a stair and which would carry it down. An old officer had stood in a hayloft four days ago and said that he no longer knew who read this town's books at night. If that man did not know, a copying clerk could hardly pretend to.

There were four names, besides, which must not be written inside a wall. He had counted an hour for them, on a ladder. What was in the case had those names nowhere in it, and it led to them all the same, as a road leads to a house.


