+# Editorial review — Movement 005, chapters 27–33
+
+## Review status and coverage
+
+This is an informed editorial model read by the review seat, with the compiled drafting history, plan, canon evidence, protected wording, and supplied measurements in context. It is not a fresh-context simulated cold read, not the author's self-review, and not a real human read.
+
+Coverage is the complete declared movement: `chapter-27.md` through `chapter-33.md`, seven chapters and 36,066 measured words. The movement is narratively complete: it enters the Deepstacks on day 284, crosses the archive week and alley attack, and leaves on day 290 with Iron Wall acquired, entered, and withheld from Vastin.
+
+## Unscored reader response
+
+Interest caught immediately on the unceremonious transfer of the lamp and the twenty-seven-step descent (`chapter-27.md:3–31`). The best sustained pleasure is the way ordinary work becomes progression: Cleon reads weight in marks, Karis builds a decipherment that can fail, and Cael learns to anticipate where the lamp must go. The movement's emotional centre is Vastin. His reflex at the yard hatch, his repeated movement toward the one available office, and his final choice to put the wall in front of people he cannot classify form one coherent and affecting change.
+
+Interest slipped most during the middle of the decipherment in `chapter-31.md:3–190`. The logic is sound and unusually fair, but the repetitions of numbers, signs, books, lamp position, and refusal to guess ask a young reader to retain more abstractions than the physical scenes do. The current manuscript also stops dead at five visible editorial markers, which are not readable prose.
+
+I expect the next event to be Karis reading enough of the language to approach the taped bundle, with Cleon's method becoming the route to Cael's next progression turn. The faction's explicit notice that it knows the loft supplies a second, external expectation. I would continue. The archive mystery, the new debt to Vastin, and the threat are all live.
+
+## Reader-lens scores
+
+Scores are editorial judgments, not audience measurements. Confidence is high for the adult-genre lens and medium for the simulated fluent-thirteen-year-old lens.
+
+| Dimension | Thirteen-year-old | Adult genre reader | Passage location, reason, confidence |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| Opening pull | 8/10 | 8/10 | `chapter-27.md:3–31`: the lamp, the unexplained count of twenty-seven, and the man descending without light create a concrete mystery before the archive is explained. High confidence. |
+| Keep reading | 8/10 | 9/10 | `chapter-27.md:171–235`, `chapter-30.md:253–279`, and `chapter-32.md:55–259`: “I know that hand,” Cleon's warning about documentation, and the closed-stair attack escalate in distinct stages and pay locally. High confidence. |
+| Interest / freshness | 9/10 | 9/10 | `chapter-29.md:175–259`: structural reading becomes a physical craft of white, black, and red counters. Bureaucratic procedure is used as danger, character, and magic rather than background lore. High confidence. |
+| Clarity / flow | 7/10 | 8/10 | `chapter-31.md:3–190`: Karis's falsifiable decipherment method is carefully explained, but the repeated number patterns and twenty-three-sign inventory are demanding on one pass. Five literal `[[TIER-B...]]` markers also break flow. Medium confidence for the younger lens; high for the textual defect. |
+| Character attachment | 9/10 | 9/10 | `chapter-28.md:113–175` and `chapter-33.md:179–231`: Cael recognizes Vastin's name as the inverse of his own stigma, then withholds the acquisition because the shared secret is not his alone. Both choices expose vulnerability without sentimentality. High confidence. |
+| Humor / warmth | 8/10 | 9/10 | `chapter-29.md:99–131`, `chapter-30.md:153–163`, and `chapter-30.md:199–251`: Brom thinks on the job only as far as the bench, “Brom was offered the life. He picked up the box,” and Vastin repeatedly tries not to become the lamp. The humor remains character-specific and stops when pain enters. High confidence. |
+| Action / suspense | 9/10 | 9/10 | `chapter-32.md:55–259`: the five flights, attackers above and below, rooted plane, loose tread, dark lantern, and Seln's cord remain spatially trackable. Each defender adapts within an injury or secrecy limit, and the minute of stillness is earned rather than padded. High confidence. |
+| Progression payoff | 9/10 | 9/10 | Plant at `chapter-29.md:283–303`, choice at `chapter-32.md:181–257`, notice at `chapter-33.md:147–173`: Cael first wants Cleon's instrument turned on him, then learns a new capability by standing behind Vastin's held line and refusing all ten existing answers. The eleventh is mechanically and emotionally distinct. High confidence. |
+| Connection | 9/10 | 9/10 | `chapter-29.md:45–131`, `chapter-32.md:111–179`, and `chapter-33.md:3–125`: Brom's old right-shoulder binding, new left-shoulder injury, Lira's recovering arm, Vastin's knee, and Karis's cut all constrain later choices and receive consequences. High confidence. |
+| Read-aloud quality | 6/10 | 7/10 | The dialogue is well attributed and most cadence is strong, especially `chapter-32.md:181–259`; however, the five markers at `chapter-27.md:221`, `chapter-28.md:73`, `chapter-29.md:219,277`, and `chapter-30.md:273` make the present files unfit for narration. The long archival explanations can also make referents harder to retain by ear. High confidence. |
+
+## Editorial findings
+
+### Blocking — five protected-text placeholders remain
+
+The manuscript is not release-ready while these literal coordinator markers remain:
+
+- `chapter-27.md:221` — Karis's “Being believed” unit.
+- `chapter-28.md:73` — Seln's truthful answer at the hatch.
+- `chapter-29.md:219` — “They're orders.”
+- `chapter-29.md:277` — “Same make.”
+- `chapter-30.md:273` — Cleon's full S11 closing speech.
+
+The first, second, and fifth markers stand for protected units whose BOOK_MAP quotations contain elisions; they require the coordinator's exact approved text, not authorial reconstruction. The two whole-line markers can be replaced verbatim with the supplied lines. Their staging and speakers fit the plan.
+
+All other protected wording applicable to this movement is present in its intended scene, including “Older than [UNBOUND]” and “I know that hand” (`chapter-27.md:177–185`), “Give me a week” (`chapter-27.md:235`), “Don't be sorry. Be nobody” and the S10 log (`chapter-28.md:105–107,291`), Cleon's two limits (`chapter-29.md:237,257`), the Seln/Brom log pair (`chapter-30.md:151,159–161`), the complete fragment-eleven notice (`chapter-33.md:156`), “I put the wall in front of it anyway” / “It's entered” (`chapter-33.md:205–207`), and the S12 closing log (`chapter-33.md:295`).
+
+### Moderate — the archive's middle asks for sustained abstract retention
+
+The movement deliberately gives the Deepstacks room, and the underlying discovery chain is good: bones, numerals, letters, then words. The most demanding stretch is `chapter-31.md:3–190`, where the reader must retain the patterns of twelve, nine, twelve, and seven; the eight known signs; and Karis's distinction between a hypothesis and a second independent spelling. A fluent thirteen-year-old can follow it, but likely with more effort than the formulas imply. One or two brief physical or relational resets inside the explanation would help audio comprehension without simplifying Karis's method or accelerating the leaf.
+
